 Workarounds
 ===========
 
+KK_WORKAROUND_21
+----------------
+| macOS version: 27.0
+| Metal ticket: Not reported
+| Metal ticket status:
+| CTS test failure: ``KHR-GLES31.core.texture_cube_map_array.image_op_vertex_sh``,
+  ``image_op_tessellation_evaluation_sh`` (through Zink)
+| Comments:
+
+The GPU kills a vertex function invocation that runs for longer than about
+10 ms and reports the submission as a timeout
+(``kIOGPUCommandBufferCallbackErrorHang``); whatever the invocation had not
+yet done is lost, and the command queue rejects every later submission. The
+limit is time per invocation: a single vertex invocation that copies a
+texture array texel by texel survives at 6 ms, is killed in some runs at
+9-11 ms and in every run from 18 ms, whatever the mix of loads, stores and
+fences, and with rasterization disabled; a million short vertices taking
+200 ms in one draw are not killed. The same loop in a one-thread compute
+dispatch runs 700 ms, and a fragment invocation 90 ms, on an Apple7 GPU
+under macOS 27.0.1 (build 26A434). The CTS cases above run one vertex or
+tessellation evaluation invocation that copies a whole cube map array image
+and land just past the limit: the kill comes after the copy and the case
+passes, but anything that slows the shader, such as Metal's shader
+validation, makes it fail.
+
+The workaround runs the stage that would be the Metal vertex function - the
+vertex shader, or the tessellation evaluation shader of a tessellation
+pipeline - as compute when it writes memory (an image or buffer store or
+atomic) anywhere and contains a loop, using the transform feedback
+pass-through with nothing to capture: the stage writes its outputs to memory
+from a compute dispatch and a generated vertex function rasterizes them.
+Pipelines with a geometry shader or transform feedback already run the stage
+as compute. A stage without a loop cannot run long enough to matter, and a
+stage that writes no memory is left alone: it loses nothing but the draw's
+rasterized output, which no realistic shader approaches the limit for, while
+routing every looping vertex shader would put ordinary shaders on the slower
+path. The criterion misses a stage that writes no memory but loops past the
+limit, and one that writes memory from straight-line code that long.
+
+The draws of a routed stage in one pass do not wait for each other, as Metal
+vertex functions of consecutive draws do not, and pin no geometry heap: a
+barrier before each draw's dispatch cost about 5 us per draw, and the heap
+reset a command buffer of its own after every pass. What remains, on an M1
+Ultra, is about 0.12 ms for each pass segment with routed draws, 0.3 us per
+draw and 1 ns per vertex. ``kosmickrisp_vertex_compute_routing`` checks the
+decision; ``kosmickrisp_long_vertex_invocation`` runs a 50 ms vertex and a
+100 ms tessellation evaluation invocation to completion, and with the
+workaround disabled ``--expect-loss`` shows them cut short;
+``kosmickrisp_vertex_compute_draws`` checks the stores and the rasterized
+output of 64 routed draws in one pass. ``MESA_KK_DEBUG=vertex_compute``
+reports every stage the workaround applies to.
+
+| Log:
+| 2026-11-02: Workaround implemented
+
 KK_WORKAROUND_20
 ----------------
 | macOS version: 27.0
